<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helena Dragaš</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1372–<a href="/years/1450">1450</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Hypomone · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helena Dragaš was a Serbian noblewoman, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-dragas">Konstantin Dragaš</a>, who in 1392 married the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-palaiologos">Manuel II Palaiologos</a>. She was the mother of the last two Byzantine emperors, John VIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, who used the surname Dragases after her family. She became a nun under the name Hypomone and is venerated as a saint by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helena-dragas.jpg" alt="Helena Dragaš" width="254" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, Byzantine period art. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helena_Dragas_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helena was born into Serbian nobility around 1372. Her father Konstantin was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyustendil">Kyustendil</a> during the fall of the Serbian Empire and was a grandson of the Serbian king Stefan III Dečanski. Her stepmother, Eudokia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond">Alexios III of Trebizond</a>. Konstantin fell at the battle of Rovine in 1395, fighting as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Bayezid I</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder">Mircea I of Wallachia</a>.</p><p>She married the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-palaiologos">Manuel II Palaiologos</a> in February 1392, and was crowned as holy empress the next day. No record explains why the marriage was arranged, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diplomatic-marriage">dynastic marriages</a> between the Byzantine and Serbian dynasties had occurred before. She was traditionally described as known for beauty, piety, wisdom and justice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Little is known of her as empress. When Manuel was absent in 1393 he made his own mother regent rather than his wife. In 1425 Manuel abdicated and became a monk, and after his death on 21 July 1425 Helena became a nun at the Monastery of Kyra Martha. She helped to found a home for old people, The Hope of the Despaired, at the Monastery of St John in Petrion.</p><p>Her vows did not take her from the court, and she is better documented as empress dowager. She advised both John VIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>. In 1439, when John VIII was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and learned that the patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> had died, he refused to name a successor until he had consulted her. She led the anti-unionist party at court that opposed union of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches, which her sons favoured, and her influence is credited with slowing it. In the end she gave her consent, or at least stopped actively opposing it, in line with John's wishes.</p><p>After John VIII died in 1448 the succession was disputed between Constantine and his younger brother Demetrios. Helena backed Constantine and served as regent until he arrived from Mistra. She persuaded Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-ii">Murad II</a> to intervene in his favour. Constantine called himself Constantine XI Dragases Palaiologos after her, the only one of her sons to take her name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Helena died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 23 March 1450. Constantine is recorded as saying that her death had deprived him of a valuable adviser. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> venerates her as Saint Hypomone, which means patience, and commemorates her on 29 May, the day of the fall of Constantinople to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and of Constantine's death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helena Dragaš” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Konstantin's daughter <b>Helena</b> married the Byzantine emperor Manuel II and was the mother of the last emperor, Constantine XI.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-dejanovic">Lordship of Dejanović</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1422" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Helena Dragaš"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1422">What was here in 1422? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
